---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The magazine system"
  description="63 in-game magazine pages plus an index, a frame, and a trophy table. Pure graphics — every word on a magazine page is baked into the pixels."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: '2D Art', href: `${base}/field-guide/2d-art/` },
    { label: 'Magazines' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The game's in-world magazines (fashion tips, town gossip, trophy lists)
    look like reading material, but technically there is <strong>no text</strong>
    inside them. Every Japanese word a player sees on a magazine page was drawn
    by Konami's art team and stored as artwork. There are no dialog strings,
    no <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO</a> entries
    holding sentences, no UTF-16 anywhere. Translating a magazine means
    editing pixels.
  </p>

  <h2>What's in the <code>magazine/</code> folder</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Count</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>magazine/MG01.ofs</code> – <code>MG63.ofs</code></td>
        <td>63</td>
        <td>One per magazine page. Each is a RESO container of pure graphics.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>magazine/magpage.ofs</code></td>
        <td>1 (66 entries)</td>
        <td>Index / table of contents pointing into the page set.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>magazine/magazine.lzs</code></td>
        <td>1</td>
        <td>The shared frame artwork (page borders, the "page X of Y" furniture).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>magazine/Trophy.ofs</code></td>
        <td>1</td>
        <td>Trophy / award listings. Same pure-graphics layout as the magazine pages.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Inside one page (<code>MGxx.ofs</code>)</h2>
  <p>
    Every <code>MGxx.ofs</code> is a RESO container whose entries are all
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/nitro-graphics/`}>Nitro graphics</a>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>NCGR</code> — the tile sheet (the actual drawn pixels).</li>
    <li><code>NCLR</code> — the palette.</li>
    <li><code>NSCR</code> — the screen-map saying which tile goes where on the page.</li>
    <li><code>NCER</code> — sprite cells for any animated bits or overlays.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is no string table. There is no opcode stream. The Japanese
    headlines, body copy, captions, and ingredient lists are all drawn
    directly into the NCGR tiles. The engine just paints the page onto the
    DS screens like any other static background.
  </p>

  <h2>What this means for translation</h2>
  <div class="fg-note">
    <strong>Magazines cannot be translated via DB / re-emit.</strong> They
    have to be redrawn. Each magazine page is essentially a one-shot
    illustration: pull the NCGR/NCLR/NSCR, render the page, paint English
    over the Japanese in an image editor, then convert the result back into
    NCGR/NSCR pairs that fit the original tile and palette constraints.
  </div>
  <p>
    This is the same problem signboards have, just scaled up: 63 pages of
    dense Japanese typography baked into the pixels. There is no shortcut,
    and the cost is image-editor labor per page. The magazine pipeline is
    therefore parked behind the main dialog translation work; it's filed as
    a long-tail polish task rather than a release blocker.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this fits in the wider archive</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/kinds-of-art/`}>Kinds of art</a> — magazines are one of the "BG-screen NSCR composites" classes.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/palettes/`}>Finding the right palette</a> — every <code>MGxx.ofs</code> ships its own NCLR sibling, so palette resolution is Tier 1.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO</a> — the container the pages live in.</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
